<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Draža Mihailović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Draža Mihailović was a Yugoslav army officer who led the royalist Chetnik movement in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and served as minister of war of the government-in-exile. After brief cooperation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s Partisans in 1941 his forces fought them, and many Chetnik units collaborated with Italian and German forces. Captured by the communists in 1946, he was tried and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/draza-mihailovic.jpg" alt="Draža Mihailović" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aleksandar Simić (1923-2007) (remastered by Savasampion) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Draza_Mihailovic,1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dragoljub Mihailović was born on 27 April 1893 in Ivanjica, in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, the son of a court clerk. Orphaned at seven, he was raised by an uncle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and joined the Serbian Military Academy in October 1910. He fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912-1913, winning the Silver Medal of Valor, served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, including the Serbian Army's retreat through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> in 1915, and received decorations on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-front">Salonika front</a>. He rose to colonel in 1935 and served as military attaché in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the German invasion of April 1941 Mihailović crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a> into German-occupied Serbia with about 80 men and in May set up at Ravna Gora, calling his force the Command of Chetnik Detachments of the Yugoslav Army. He aimed to unite Serb bands and build an organisation able to take power once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> withdrew, rather than to confront the occupiers directly, fearing German reprisals against civilians and a Partisan takeover. By late 1941 his forces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s Partisans were in open conflict, and by September he was losing men to the Partisans. On 11 January 1942 the government-in-exile named him Minister of the Army, Navy and Air Forces, and in June he became chief of staff of the Supreme Command.</p><p>Chetnik ideology included a Greater Serbia, and Chetnik forces carried out massacres and used terror to drive out non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, partly in response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> violence. Many Chetnik groups collaborated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>, and British officers reported his reluctance to attack the occupiers. Operation Halyard, in 1944, rescued hundreds of downed Allied airmen with his help. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, however, shifted its support to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>, and on 29 August 1944 King Peter dissolved Mihailović's command and on 12 September called on all to join Tito's army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Yugoslav authorities captured Mihailović on 13 March 1946, by one account by luring him onto a supposed British evacuation aircraft, by another through betrayal by Nikola Kalabić. His trial opened on 10 June, and the Allied airmen he had rescued were not allowed to testify. He was convicted of high treason and war crimes and shot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 17 July 1946. In his final statement he said, &quot;I wanted much; I began much; but the gale of the world carried away me and my work.&quot;</p><p>Historians still disagree about his responsibility for collaboration and massacres. Jozo Tomasevich and Pavlowitch stress his failure to grow into his responsibilities and his limited control over local commanders. In May 2015 the High Court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> rehabilitated him, ruling that the communist trial had been politically and ideologically motivated; the request had drawn protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Draža Mihailović” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government-in-exile backed <b>Draža Mihailović</b>’s Chetniks, but Tito’s Partisans won the war, and a constituent assembly abolished the monarchy on 29 November 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with <b>Draža Mihailović's</b> Chetniks broke down into open fighting in early November 1941.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-uzice">Republic of Užice</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ivanjica, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.58,20.23&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Ivanjica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5036 619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Draža Mihailović"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5486 889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5486 889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.58,20.23&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
